  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1204 AM MDT Sat Oct 3 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThrough the weekend, an upper-level ridge will build and make its \nway east towards Montana keeping the region warm and dry. A weak \nshortwave will move through Monday into Tuesday and flatten the \nridge a bit which, combined with increased mid-level flow, will \nallow for breezy conditions along the Rocky Mountain Front into the \nnorthern plains on Monday. As the disturbance passes, temperatures \nwill cool off about 10 degrees starting on Tuesday but still remain \nabove normal as upper-level ridging quickly builds back through the \nend of the work week. Towards next weekend there are hints in the\nmodels that a pattern shift could arrive with the potential for \ncooler and wetter weather. But with models still diverging on how \nexactly this will play out, confidence in any one scenario is \nstill low.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThere is high confidence in temperatures reaching the low 80s across \nmany lower elevation locations on Monday with some locations, \nincluding Great Falls and Helena, expected to get within 5 degrees \nof the daily record. The warm temperatures combined with breezy \nconditions will increase fire weather concerns for the afternoon but \ngiven that the fuel condition is not critical, no products will be \nconsidered at this time.\n\nRegarding the potential pattern shift next weekend, there have been \nsome runs of both the GFS and ECMWF that have pointed towards \nmountain snow with an occasional run suggesting even some lower \nelevation snow in Southwest Montana. Because there is still a bit of \na \"windshield wiper\" effect going on between model runs, with one \nrun being more robust on the snow potential and the next one taking \nit away, it is highly recommended that folks take any mention of \nsnow with a grain of salt until we get closer to next weekend. A \npattern shift towards cooler and wetter conditions is possible, but \nconfidence in any one scenario is low. -thor",
